# The patches are applied by GNU patch against sources extracted from the
# pinned tarball, which uses LF throughout. Git for Windows defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true and would rewrite these files to CRLF on checkout, leaving
# every context line with a trailing CR: hunks then apply "with fuzz", meaning
# patch guessed at the location, or fail outright. Pin them.
*.patch text eol=lf

# Verification and build scripts run under bash, which does not tolerate CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Files copied verbatim from the other half of this project, so that
# tests/test_team_preset.py can compare against them without their repository
# checked out. Their whole value is being byte-for-byte what upstream holds:
# rewriting the line endings on checkout would break the blob hash recorded in
# reference/README.md, which is the only way to prove the copy was not edited.
integrations/torcs-1.3.9/reference/** -text
